# Line endings: one canonical tree for every git client.
#
# `core.autocrlf` is per-client, not per-repository. The Windows (PowerShell)
# client runs with `autocrlf=true` and normalises on commit, while a Linux/WSL
# checkout runs with `autocrlf=false` and does neither — so the 84 files stored
# with LF in the index were reported as "modified" in WSL while Windows called
# the same tree clean. `text=auto` makes every client normalise the worktree
# before comparing or hashing, so both agree without rewriting anyone's global
# git config.
#
# No `eol=` is pinned for text: checkouts follow `core.eol` (`native`), which
# keeps CRLF on Windows and LF elsewhere.
* text=auto

# Notebooks are JSON and must stay diffable, so they are text — but pinned to LF
# so the embedded base64 payloads cannot churn the whole file between clients.
*.ipynb text eol=lf

# Binary payloads: never normalised, never diffed as text.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.webp binary
*.pdf binary
*.zip binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
